  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 248 AM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nGeneral zonal flow will prevail by midweek across much of CONUS as \nbroad upper ridging resides across the south and enhanced mid-level \nflow from a deep trough tracks along the US/Canadian border. In \ntime, upper ridging will retrograde and settle over the Desert \nSouthwest and may introduce some subtle northwesterly flow late \nweek. Departing frontal boundary from Monday/Tuesday will quell \nnorthward advance of richer low-level moisture for a few days and \nthis will coincide with high pressure in the region. Cooler \ntemperatures will result with NBM inner quartile values falling to \nthe lower 80s by Wednesday with a steady increase towards Friday. \nPrecipitation chances will be sparse in this time frame with \nensembles fairly pessimistic on wetting rains anywhere in the \nregion. Deeper troughing will move onshore the Pacific Northwest \nsometime over the weekend. This feature will dig south towards the \nGreat Basin before crossing the central/northern Rockies sometime \nearly next week. In the lead up to this, southwesterly flow will \nbecome established again and will lead to an increase in atmospheric \nmoisture with PWATs advertised to exceed 140% of normal. This \nsuggests a cloudy and cooler period with greater rain potential. NBM \nensemble spreads highlight this well with inner-quartile values \nshowing increasing spread through the weekend towards Monday where \nvalues at North Platte reaches 21F. QPF probabilities show similar \nincreases though fall short of high confidence with only 33%, 35%, \nand 23% probabilities for seeing > 0.10\" rainfall each day Saturday, \nSunday, and Monday respectively. So even though rain chances \nincrease towards the end of the period, confidence in truly \nbeneficial rainfall remains low at this time.",
